<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · date unknown · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Nasadiya Sukta (Hymn of Creation)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="sa">नासदीय सूक्त</bdi> / <bdi lang="sa-Latn">Nāsadīya Sūkta</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An ancient Indian hymn that wonders how the universe began, and admits that perhaps no one knows.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>a Vedic seer (rishi), not named</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Vedic tradition of ancient India</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vedic Sanskrit</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>a late hymn of the Rigveda, in its 10th book</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; a hymn of the Rigveda)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The Nāsadīya Sūkta, also called the Hymn of Creation, is the 129th hymn of the 10th book (mandala) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> (10.129). Its name comes from its opening words. It is concerned with cosmology and the origin of the universe. Like the 10th book as a whole, it is late within the Rigveda, and it expresses thought more typical of later Vedantic philosophy. Although it is unlike most Vedic hymns, it is one of the most widely received parts of the Rigveda, and it has drawn a large literature of commentary both in the Indian schools of philosophy (darśanas) and in Western philology.</p><p>The hymn asks when, why and through whom the universe came into being, and gives no definite answer. It suggests that the gods themselves may not know, since they came after creation, and that even the one who watches over creation from the highest heaven may or may not know. For this reason the usual English title is perhaps misleading: the hymn does not tell a creation myth, but leads the listener to ask whether anyone can know how the universe began. As it unfolds, it moves from a state before both being and non-being towards being, naming heat (tapas) and desire (kāma) among the first causes, and the first poet-seers (kavayas) as those who sought the origin in their own thought.</p><p>It is composed in seven stanzas in the trishtubh metre, but one line of the last stanza is two syllables short, and the final line ends in a clause left without a main clause. The scholar Brereton (1999) argued that both are deliberate, a way for the seer to express puzzlement at the thought that the world may not have been created, and that the hymn's slow passage from non-being to being re-enacts creation within the listener, equating poetic speech with creation. Karel Werner described its source as not reasoning but &quot;visionary, mystical or Yogic experience put into words&quot;.</p><p>An atheist reading sees the hymn as one of the earliest accounts of sceptical inquiry and agnosticism. The astronomer Carl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagan">Sagan</a> quoted it when he discussed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s &quot;tradition of skeptical questioning and unselfconscious humility before the great cosmic mysteries.&quot;</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A meditation on the beginning of the universe. It pictures the state before anything existed, traces how being may have arisen, and ends by doubting whether even the gods, or the highest overseer, know how it happened. The mood is quiet, questioning and humble.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Vedic Sanskrit</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Vedic Sanskrit</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">नासदासीन्नो सदासीत्तदानीं नासीद्रजो नो व्योमा परो यत्।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">nāsad āsīn no sad āsīt tadānīṃ nāsīd rajo no vyomā paro yat |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Non-being was not, nor was being then; there was no airy space, nor the sky which is beyond.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">किमावरीवः कुह कस्य शर्मन्नम्भः किमासीद्गहनं गभीरम्॥१॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kim āvarīvaḥ kuha kasya śarmann ambhaḥ kim āsīd gahanaṃ gabhīram || 1 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What stirred? Where? In whose shelter? Was there water, deep, unfathomable? 1</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">न मृत्युरासीदमृतं न तर्हि न रात्र्या अह्न आसीत्प्रकेतः।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">na mṛtyur āsīd amṛtaṃ na tarhi na rātryā ahna āsīt praketaḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Death was not, nor deathlessness then; there was no sign of night or of day.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">आनीदवातं स्वधया तदेकं तस्माद्धान्यन्न परः किं चनास॥२॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">ānīd avātaṃ svadhayā tad ekaṃ tasmād dhānyan na paraḥ kiṃ canāsa || 2 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That One breathed, windless, by its own power; other than that there was nothing at all beyond. 2</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">तम आसीत्तमसा गूळ्हमग्रेऽप्रकेतं सलिलं सर्वमा इदम्।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">tama āsīt tamasā gūḷham agre 'praketaṃ salilaṃ sarvam ā idam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Darkness there was, hidden by darkness, in the beginning; all this was water without a sign.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">तुच्छ्येनाभ्वपिहितं यदासीत्तपसस्तन्महिनाजायतैकम्॥३॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">tucchyenābhv apihitaṃ yad āsīt tapasas tan mahinājāyataikam || 3 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What was coming to be, covered by emptiness, that One was born by the power of heat. 3</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">कामस्तदग्रे समवर्तताधि मनसो रेतः प्रथमं यदासीत्।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kāmas tad agre sam avartatādhi manaso retaḥ prathamaṃ yad āsīt |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Desire came upon it in the beginning, which was the first seed of mind.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">सतो बन्धुमसति निरविन्दन् हृदि प्रतीष्या कवयो मनीषा॥४॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">sato bandhum asati nir avindan hṛdi pratīṣyā kavayo manīṣā || 4 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The poets, searching in the heart with insight, found the bond of being in non-being. 4</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">तिरश्चीनो विततो रश्मिरेषामधः स्विदासी३दुपरि स्विदासी३त्।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">tiraścīno vitato raśmir eṣām adhaḥ svid āsī3d upari svid āsī3t |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Their cord was stretched across: was there a below? was there an above?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">रेतोधा आसन्महिमान आसन्त्स्वधा अवस्तात्प्रयतिः परस्तात्॥५॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">retodhā āsan mahimāna āsant svadhā avastāt prayatiḥ parastāt || 5 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There were seed-placers, there were powers; own-power below, giving forth above. 5</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">को अद्धा वेद क इह प्र वोचत्कुत आजाता कुत इयं विसृष्टिः।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">ko addhā veda ka iha pra vocat kuta ājātā kuta iyaṃ visṛṣṭiḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who truly knows? Who shall declare it here: from where it was born, from where this creation?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">अर्वाग्देवा अस्य विसर्जनेनाथा को वेद यत आबभूव॥६॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">arvāg devā asya visarjanenāthā ko veda yata ābabhūva || 6 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The gods are later than the creation of this; then who knows from where it came into being? 6</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">इयं विसृष्टिर्यत आबभूव यदि वा दधे यदि वा न।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">iyaṃ visṛṣṭir yata ābabhūva yadi vā dadhe yadi vā na |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This creation, from where it came into being, whether it was set in place or whether not —</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">यो अस्याध्यक्षः परमे व्योमन्त्सो अङ्ग वेद यदि वा न वेद॥७॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">yo asyādhyakṣaḥ parame vyomant so aṅga veda yadi vā na veda || 7 ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he who is its overseer in the highest heaven, he surely knows; or perhaps he does not know. 7</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Rigveda 10.129. Text from <a href="https://sa.wikisource.org/wiki/%E0%A4%8B%E0%A4%97%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%B5%E0%A5%87%E0%A4%A6%E0%A4%83_%E0%A4%B8%E0%A5%82%E0%A4%95%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%A4%E0%A4%82_%E0%A5%A7%E0%A5%A6.%E0%A5%A7%E0%A5%A8%E0%A5%AF" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (IAST).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (9)</summary><ul><li>Each stanza is one verse in two half-lines; the original's verse numbers are kept at the end of the second half-line.</li><li>1.1: 'rajas' here is the middle region, the airy space between earth and sky.</li><li>1.2: 'āvarīvaḥ' is disputed: 'what covered (it)?' or 'what stirred / moved to and fro?'.</li><li>2.2: 'svadhā', 'own power' or 'self-will'; 'tad ekam', 'that One'.</li><li>3.2: 'ābhu' is disputed: 'what was coming into being', 'the void', 'the potential'; 'tapas' is heat, also ascetic fervour.</li><li>5.1: 'āsī3t' carries the pluti (a prolonged vowel, the numeral 3 in the original), marking a question; 'raśmi' is a cord or ray.</li><li>5.2: 'prayati' is disputed: 'impulse', 'giving forth', 'offering'.</li><li>7.1: 'dadhe' may be 'he/it established (it)' or 'it was established': whether it was made or not.</li><li>7.2: 'aṅga' is an emphatic particle ('surely, indeed').</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
